{
  "source": "Parsed exactly from this walk-left-cycle (1).svg's <text> coordinates (the SECOND file with this exact name) -- supersedes the previous walk-left-cycle (1).svg (the eye-position fix). New art pass, same technique, with one change worth flagging: lefthead's own @keyframes now bob -20, 0, -20, 0px across the 4 quarters, i.e. the OPPOSITE phase from every earlier walk-left bake (which started at 0px, not -20px) -- read straight from the file's own keyframe rule, not hand-corrected, so the bob's up/down parity intentionally follows whatever this reference specifies. Same two-part technique as every walk-left bake so far: a single always-visible <g class=\"lefthead\"> (hat through shoulders/upper torso) that bobs via its own @keyframes translateY (-20, 0, -20, 0px across the 4 quarters), painted OVER four opacity-toggled <g style=\"animation: leftb0..3 ...\"> frame blocks (legs + lower torso, fixed position, never translate -- only which one is visible changes per quarter, steps(1,end)/0.64s cadence). \"lefthead\" already covers the whole upper figure rather than a head-only stub, so bobbing it as one piece keeps head and torso moving together with no seam. Both lefthead's own bottom row and the top row of each leftb group draw an identical duplicate collar line, so that seam has valid content whether or not the head is bobbed up out of the way. No ground-normalization needed: the legs never change vertical position, only which pre-drawn pose is showing. Baked into the same 4-frame shape by sampling lefthead's own @keyframes translateY at t=0/25/50/75% and pairing it with whichever leftb group is visible that quarter, padded 1 row(s) at the top for the head's upward shift. Colours clustered across all five groups together down to 40 (custom JS k-means++, no deps) from 548 near-unique source hexes, then every column doubled for this game's non-square ~1.667:1 (tall:wide) character cell — see PLAYER's comment in sprites.ts for the same fix.",
